WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.020 --> 00:00:04.400
Since its inception, Twitch has been
responsible for creating moments that

00:00:04.400 --> 00:00:05.740
we'll remember forever.

00:00:06.400 --> 00:00:07.800
Shroud: So, listen...

00:00:09.540 --> 00:00:11.000
I'm gonna hide the evidence...

00:00:11.000 --> 00:00:11.700
...alright?

00:00:11.700 --> 00:00:12.320
Screaming child: Nooooo!

00:00:13.980 --> 00:00:16.520
[screaming child]

00:00:20.100 --> 00:00:23.620
Drama, sadness and hype, all abound in the broadcasted

00:00:23.620 --> 00:00:25.300
lives of esports pros,

00:00:25.300 --> 00:00:26.620
streamers

00:00:26.620 --> 00:00:27.120
and even special one-offs like Twitch

00:00:27.120 --> 00:00:29.280
Presents.

00:00:29.280 --> 00:00:31.820
Kappa dominates sarcasm

00:00:31.820 --> 00:00:33.880
Pogchamp and TriHard bring the hype

00:00:33.880 --> 00:00:36.380
and Biblethump harnesses our sorrow

00:00:36.380 --> 00:00:39.700
However, when it comes to humor – a staple of

00:00:39.700 --> 00:00:44.820
Twitch – no emote dominates so completely as LUL.

00:00:44.820 --> 00:00:46.080
But, believe it or not,

00:00:46.080 --> 00:00:47.140
LUL wasn't even

00:00:47.140 --> 00:00:50.700
an official Twitch global emote until recently.

00:00:50.700 --> 00:00:52.180
To find its origins we

00:00:52.180 --> 00:00:54.340
have to go back to 2013.

00:00:54.340 --> 00:00:57.620
LUL actually started life as a photograph of John

00:00:57.620 --> 00:01:01.920
"TotalBiscuit" Bain – a popular figure in
the video game community.

00:01:02.080 --> 00:01:03.100
Now known for game

00:01:03.100 --> 00:01:07.320
reviews and his love of both Starcraft and Warcraft,

00:01:07.320 --> 00:01:08.700
Bain's involvement in gaming

00:01:08.700 --> 00:01:10.700
goes back into the distant past...

00:01:10.700 --> 00:01:12.240
TotalBiscuit: This is World of Warcraft Radio,

00:01:12.240 --> 00:01:13.620
my name is TotalBiscuit

00:01:13.620 --> 00:01:16.220
and I have to fill two hours with talk

00:01:16.220 --> 00:01:17.700
and general drivel.

00:01:17.700 --> 00:01:20.080
Josh: At MLG Anaheim 2013,

00:01:20.080 --> 00:01:22.740
event photographer Jonathan Tayag

00:01:22.740 --> 00:01:26.780
also known as "itsjustatank" took this now famous

00:01:26.780 --> 00:01:28.060
image of TotalBiscuit.

00:01:28.060 --> 00:01:29.800
The image would come to be used

00:01:29.800 --> 00:01:34.040
on TotalBiscuit's Twitch stream as a sub emote called

00:01:34.040 --> 00:01:34.660
"Cynical Laugh".

00:01:34.660 --> 00:01:38.000
However, Tank later claimed that the image was used without his

00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:41.520
permission and he issued a takedown
request for the original image

00:01:41.520 --> 00:01:43.120
in July of 2014.

00:01:43.140 --> 00:01:45.520
Bain claimed Tank made no attempt to

00:01:45.520 --> 00:01:48.940
contact him before issuing the takedown request

00:01:48.940 --> 00:01:51.180
and would have gladly paid for rights.

00:01:51.180 --> 00:01:53.960
Tank said his issue was that the emote

00:01:53.960 --> 00:01:55.480
was hidden behind a paywall,

00:01:55.480 --> 00:01:57.700
it was a sub emote only, after all.

00:01:57.700 --> 00:02:00.640
Had it been free for all to use, he said he would

00:02:00.640 --> 00:02:01.660
have had no issue.

00:02:01.960 --> 00:02:04.660
Now here's where things get really interesting.

00:02:04.660 --> 00:02:08.860
While "CynicalLaugh" was gone as a sub
emote for the moment,

00:02:08.860 --> 00:02:10.280
it was also added

00:02:10.280 --> 00:02:11.880
to Better Twitch TV.

00:02:12.160 --> 00:02:15.600
Better Twitch TV is a browser add-on that enables new

00:02:15.600 --> 00:02:17.900
emotes on Twitch, but you only see them if

00:02:17.900 --> 00:02:20.180
you have the add-on installed.

00:02:20.340 --> 00:02:21.340
If you don't,

00:02:21.340 --> 00:02:23.500
all you see is the command used.

00:02:23.500 --> 00:02:26.800
The emote's command was "LUL"

00:02:26.800 --> 00:02:29.740
and because anyone can install BTTV,

00:02:29.740 --> 00:02:32.320
that meant the emote was available to everyone.

00:02:32.320 --> 00:02:35.900
So the add-on was able to use the original, contested

00:02:35.900 --> 00:02:38.680
CynicalLaugh image that Tank had taken,

00:02:38.680 --> 00:02:40.880
and it was through Better Twitch TV

00:02:40.880 --> 00:02:43.840
that the emote and command became popular.

00:02:43.840 --> 00:02:47.540
Chris Farley: Well, La-dee-FRICKIN-da!

00:02:47.540 --> 00:02:50.500
Josh: Why name it "LUL" in the first place though?

00:02:50.720 --> 00:02:54.580
The term had begun to gain popularity as a distortion

00:02:54.580 --> 00:02:57.620
or variation of L-O-L, or

00:02:57.620 --> 00:02:58.760
Laughing Out Loud.

00:02:58.760 --> 00:03:01.120
Some sources suggest that it stands for

00:03:01.120 --> 00:03:03.540
"Lame Uncomfortable Laugh",

00:03:03.540 --> 00:03:05.640
which seems pretty unlikely.

00:03:05.640 --> 00:03:09.500
My favorite, is that if you say L-O-L– "lol"

00:03:09.500 --> 00:03:13.860
out loud it usually sounds more like "lul".

00:03:13.860 --> 00:03:17.460
The term "lulz" and "for the lulz" had

00:03:17.460 --> 00:03:20.100
previously been connected with hacking group

00:03:20.100 --> 00:03:22.980
Anonymous and internet culture generally

00:03:22.980 --> 00:03:25.100
as early as 2007.

00:03:25.360 --> 00:03:28.400
It meant doing something just for the laughs or

00:03:28.400 --> 00:03:29.900
just for the hell of it.

00:03:30.240 --> 00:03:34.180
Of course the media back then was a lot less savvy to

00:03:34.180 --> 00:03:37.280
internet culture and so we got reports,

00:03:37.280 --> 00:03:38.440
like this...

00:03:38.440 --> 00:03:40.420
Reporter: They attack innocent people

00:03:40.420 --> 00:03:42.300
like an internet hate machine.

00:03:42.300 --> 00:03:46.420
???: They get laughs, they enjoy doing this..

00:03:46.420 --> 00:03:48.660
They get what they call "LULs".

00:03:48.660 --> 00:03:50.660
Reporter: But truly epic LULs

00:03:50.660 --> 00:03:52.420
come from raids and invasions

00:03:52.420 --> 00:03:55.520
branded on the Anonymous website with an "I".

00:03:55.640 --> 00:03:57.340
Like their nationwide campaign

00:03:57.340 --> 00:04:00.100
to spoil the new Harry Potter book ending.

00:04:04.320 --> 00:04:06.040
Josh: The connotation that LUL has

00:04:06.040 --> 00:04:10.680
implies that you're laughing at someone
or something specific

00:04:12.100 --> 00:04:13.500
It's not really a general laughter,

00:04:13.500 --> 00:04:17.820
but more often targeted and often with a generous

00:04:17.820 --> 00:04:19.500
amount of cynicism.

00:04:19.500 --> 00:04:22.280
Perfect for the cynical Brit
himself, TotalBiscuit

00:04:22.620 --> 00:04:25.260
Another thing that's definitely true about LUL,

00:04:25.260 --> 00:04:27.680
is that it means "dick" in Dutch.

00:04:27.860 --> 00:04:30.680
I'm not kidding, it means a big ol'

00:04:30.680 --> 00:04:32.220
Carnival Worker: One-eyed monster,

00:04:32.220 --> 00:04:33.420
step right up and see

00:04:33.420 --> 00:04:34.900
the one-eyed monster.

00:04:34.900 --> 00:04:35.920
Josh: Anyways,

00:04:35.920 --> 00:04:38.400
LUL had a greater destiny than simply

00:04:38.400 --> 00:04:41.300
remaining a BTTV emote.

00:04:41.300 --> 00:04:45.680
In August 2016, TotalBiscuit teased "CynicalLUL" –

00:04:45.680 --> 00:04:48.040
a new version of the emote on Twitter.

00:04:48.040 --> 00:04:50.480
It featured a stylized depiction of the expression

00:04:50.480 --> 00:04:53.080
seen in the original photograph,

00:04:53.080 --> 00:04:54.840
thus avoiding further legal

00:04:54.840 --> 00:04:56.740
issues with the photographer.

00:04:56.740 --> 00:05:00.840
But the real coup came on September 1st 2017,

00:05:00.840 --> 00:05:03.600
when LUL was made a global emote.

00:05:03.600 --> 00:05:06.400
It should have been a big moment for the

00:05:06.400 --> 00:05:07.400
emote...

00:05:07.400 --> 00:05:08.440
The problem?

00:05:08.440 --> 00:05:11.620
Twitch used TotalBiscuit's new, 2016 version

00:05:11.620 --> 00:05:12.820
of the emote

00:05:12.820 --> 00:05:16.900
and not the LUL that BTTV fans were used to

00:05:16.900 --> 00:05:19.040
and because it used the same command

00:05:19.040 --> 00:05:20.200
L-U-L

00:05:20.200 --> 00:05:23.520
it caused some disruption with fans.

00:05:23.520 --> 00:05:25.800
TotalBiscuit explained that due to the

00:05:25.800 --> 00:05:27.900
previous dispute with the photo,

00:05:27.900 --> 00:05:31.220
Twitch wouldn't use the original and to his

00:05:31.220 --> 00:05:33.300
credit he took some enjoyment from the

00:05:33.300 --> 00:05:35.800
outrage, offering up this new subscriber

00:05:35.800 --> 00:05:40.360
emote that used the rallying cry of BTTV LUL loyalists:

00:05:40.920 --> 00:05:42.920
Not My LUL.

00:05:42.920 --> 00:05:43.540
Thankfully,

00:05:43.540 --> 00:05:46.160
Twitch revolution was averted when the original LUL

00:05:46.160 --> 00:05:49.640
emote was reinstated as L-u-L.

00:05:49.640 --> 00:05:53.040
That's right... Capital Ls, small U.

00:05:53.040 --> 00:05:54.420
Peter Weller: Well, halle-f***ing-lujah!

00:05:54.860 --> 00:05:56.400
Josh: And that's the story of LUL.

00:05:56.400 --> 00:05:58.920
But one thing that always bothered me is

00:05:58.920 --> 00:06:01.840
whether everyone using LUL in chat

00:06:01.840 --> 00:06:05.280
even understood that it was a BTTV emote?

00:06:05.280 --> 00:06:09.240
What percentage of viewers simply parroted LUL understanding it

00:06:09.240 --> 00:06:11.940
instinctively as a form of LOL

00:06:11.940 --> 00:06:14.860
without BTTV actually installed?

00:06:14.860 --> 00:06:16.460
We'll never know for sure,

00:06:16.460 --> 00:06:18.580
but if you're one of those people...

00:06:18.580 --> 00:06:19.980
Nice work,

00:06:19.980 --> 00:06:20.940
LUL.

00:06:21.960 --> 00:06:24.040
Miles: What the f*** is with these endings?

00:06:24.040 --> 00:06:26.180
Every week it gets worse and worse,

00:06:26.180 --> 00:06:26.680
Josh: I thought –

00:06:26.680 --> 00:06:27.880
Miles: Like what the f*** is wrong with you, dude?

00:06:29.900 --> 00:06:31.900
Josh: I wish people could understand

00:06:31.900 --> 00:06:34.060
that the setup is like the camera and
your face

00:06:34.060 --> 00:06:36.400
it is right next to the lens, basically.

00:06:36.400 --> 00:06:38.300
But in the background, so like
I can immediately see if

00:06:38.300 --> 00:06:39.240
I f***ed up.

00:06:39.240 --> 00:06:40.420
Like there's no time...

00:06:40.420 --> 00:06:41.760
Miles: I haven't got a very good poker face either...

00:06:41.760 --> 00:06:43.720
Josh: No, you're like...

00:06:44.160 --> 00:06:44.920
[laughter]

00:06:44.920 --> 00:06:47.000
Josh: Like somebody's needling you or

00:06:47.000 --> 00:06:49.020
like bamboo under the fingernails.

00:06:49.020 --> 00:06:50.000
[laughter]

00:06:50.000 --> 00:06:53.000
Josh: Ok.

